  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nOvernight rain showers continue to stream across the Snake \nPlain and Eastern Highlands early this morning. High-resolution \nconvective-allowing models (CAMs) show this initial wave exiting\nby mid-morning, yielding a brief lull in precipitation. \nHowever, incoming upper-level shortwave energy and lingering \natmospheric moisture will spark a secondary round of showers and\nthunderstorms by early-to-mid afternoon. While forcing and QPF \nwill focus heavily over the Central Mountains and along the \nContinental Divide, latest model trends indicate an isolated \nshower or storm is possible nearly anywhere across eastern \nIdaho. Given the scattered nature of lightning strikes on dry \nfuels, a Red Flag Warning remains active for Fire Zone 422 \ntoday. Convective activity will taper off late this evening, \nwith most showers diminishing by midnight.\n\nBroad upper-level troughing anchored over the Pacific Northwest\nwill dictate regional weather through the remainder of the \nworkweek. Embedded impulses rotating around the parent low will \ntrack through the flow, with one prominent lobe crossing the \nregion on Wednesday. Because the core energy will remain shunted\nto our north and west, shower and thunderstorm chances \nWednesday and Thursday will be largely restricted to the Central\nMountains. The more widespread operational impact mid-week will\nbe strong afternoon winds driven by a tight regional pressure \ngradient. Several wind-prone valley corridors will flirt with \nWind Advisory thresholds each afternoon. Fortunately, \nunseasonably cool air will hold minimum relative humidity values\nabove critical thresholds. Afternoon highs Wednesday through \nFriday will top out in the mid-to-upper 70s in lower elevations \nand mid-60s to lower 70s across mountain zones.\n\nLower elevations may see a brief temperature recovery into the\nlower 80s on Saturday before the next weather system approaches.\nGuidance indicates a potentially unsettled pattern taking hold\nfor the latter half of the Labor Day holiday weekend, with rain\nchances trending upward Sunday into Monday. While global models\ncontinue to struggle with the exact synoptic placement of the\nupper low and deepest moisture plumes, confidence remains high\nthat below-normal temperatures will persist through much of the\nfirst week of September.",
